{"claims":[{"excerpt":"Who Counts as Unemployed?","claim":"The piece explains which people are classified as unemployed.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Under the Current Population Survey, an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week, was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall.","narrative":"Explain who is counted as unemployed—and therefore included in the unemployment-rate denominator—through two contrasting situations: a temporarily laid-off person expecting recall and someone who only browsed job listings.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The title accurately describes the subject of the source and narrative.","materiality":"The title sets the piece’s scope."},{"excerpt":"The unemployment rate is calculated using the number of unemployed people divided by the combined number of employed and unemployed people","claim":"The unemployment rate’s denominator is the sum of employed and unemployed people.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the sum of employed and unemployed people.","narrative":"Establish that the unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the combined number of employed and unemployed people, rather than by everyone without a job.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The stated denominator matches the source.","materiality":"This is a central definition of the rate."},{"excerpt":"not everyone who lacks a job","claim":"The unemployment-rate denominator does not include everyone without a job.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the sum of employed and unemployed people.","narrative":"rather than by everyone without a job.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source defines the denominator as employed plus unemployed people, not all people lacking a job.","materiality":"This clarifies the population used in the rate."},{"excerpt":"Being jobless alone is not enough to be classified as unemployed.","claim":"Lacking a job alone does not establish that someone is classified as unemployed.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Under the Current Population Survey, an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week, was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall.","narrative":"The reader should understand that unemployment is not simply the absence of a job: classification depends on being available and, in most cases, actively searching during the specified period.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source lists additional conditions beyond not being employed.","materiality":"This distinction is the piece’s central point."},{"excerpt":"A person must not have been employed during the survey’s reference week","claim":"A person must not have been employed during the survey reference week to meet the stated unemployed-person definition.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Under the Current Population Survey, an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week","narrative":"the person must not have been employed during the reference week","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The reference-week condition is accurately stated.","materiality":"It is a defining eligibility condition."},{"excerpt":"must have been available for work, except in the case of temporary illness","claim":"The person must have been available for work, with temporary illness as an exception.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"was available for work except for temporary illness","narrative":"must have been available for work, except for temporary illness","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The exception and availability condition match the source.","materiality":"It is a defining eligibility condition."},{"excerpt":"In most cases, the person must also have actively searched for work during the four weeks ending with that reference week.","claim":"In most cases, the person must have actively searched for work during the four-week period ending with the reference week.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall. Passive-only job search does not count as active search.","narrative":"Active search must fall within the four-week period ending with the reference week, unless the person is temporarily laid off and expects recall.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The timeframe and qualification are consistent with the source’s active-search requirement and temporary-layoff exception.","materiality":"The search requirement and period affect classification."},{"excerpt":"There is an exception for someone on temporary layoff who expects to be recalled.","claim":"A temporarily laid-off person expecting recall is an exception to the active-search requirement.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"or was temporarily laid off and expected recall","narrative":"a temporarily laid-off person expecting recall","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source explicitly provides the temporary-layoff-and-recall alternative to active search.","materiality":"This exception is a central contrast in the piece."},{"excerpt":"That person may qualify as unemployed without actively searching, provided the other conditions are met.","claim":"A temporarily laid-off person expecting recall may qualify without active search if the other conditions are met.","category":"certainty","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall.","narrative":"The first may qualify without an active search","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source presents temporary layoff with expected recall as an alternative to active search; the other listed conditions still apply.","materiality":"This describes the exception without implying that all unemployed-person conditions are waived."},{"excerpt":"someone whose only job-search activity was browsing listings has not actively searched","claim":"Browsing listings alone does not count as active job search.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Passive-only job search does not count as active search.","narrative":"someone whose only job-search activity was browsing listings. ... passive browsing alone does not meet the active-search requirement.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source says passive-only search does not count as active; the narrative identifies browsing listings as the example.","materiality":"It distinguishes passive activity from qualifying active search."},{"excerpt":"does not qualify on that basis","claim":"A person whose only search activity was browsing listings does not meet the active-search route to unemployment classification.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Passive-only job search does not count as active search.","narrative":"passive browsing alone does not meet the active-search requirement.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"Read in context, “on that basis” refers to qualifying through the active-search requirement, not the separate temporary-layoff exception.","materiality":"The qualification prevents t